Comment (internal/service/relational/catalog.go:108):
**Low — duplicate `catalog-type` prop on OSCAL round-trip.** `UnmarshalOscal` (catalog.go:50-59) stores metadata props verbatim into `Metadata.Props` *and* derives `CatalogType` from those same props. So a policy/procedure catalog imported **with** a `catalog-type` prop in its body — the documented round-trip path — persists that prop in metadata; on marshal `c.Metadata.MarshalOscal()` re-emits it and then this block appends a **second** identical prop. Output ends up with two `catalog-type` props, contradicting the "reads it straight back / non-standard types ride a metadata prop" round-trip guarantee.

The unit tests only cover the column-only case (no prop in the source metadata), so they miss this. Suggest deduping — e.g. strip any existing `catalog-type` prop before appending here, or drop it from `Metadata.Props` in `UnmarshalOscal` so the column is the sole source. Minor for a PoC, but the prop mechanism exists precisely for clean round-tripping.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Good catch — fixing. In `Catalog.MarshalOscal`, before appending the catalog-type prop, strip any pre-existing `catalog-type` prop (name==catalogTypePropName && ns==CCFPropNamespace) from the copied metadata props, so a catalog imported *with* the prop in its body doesn't end up with two. Adding a round-trip unit test whose source metadata already carries a catalog-type prop, asserting exactly one after marshal.

